    <article class="prose">
      <section id="system" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">01 / The system</p>
        <h2>Eleven words, modelled from the waveform up.</h2>
        <p class="lead">The aim was not to call a speech-recognition API. It was to implement the recogniser’s central algorithms and see their assumptions directly.</p>
        <p>The system recognises an eleven-word vocabulary of acoustically similar utterances. Raw audio is converted into 13-dimensional Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients, giving a compact representation of how the signal’s spectral content changes through time.</p>
        <p>Each word is represented by a left-to-right, eight-state Hidden Markov Model. Baum–Welch expectation–maximisation learns the transition probabilities and diagonal Gaussian emission distributions. At evaluation time, Viterbi decoding finds the most likely state path under every word model and selects the highest-scoring word.</p>
        <div class="schedule" role="img" aria-label="Speech recognition pipeline: raw audio, MFCC features, HMM training, Viterbi decoding">
          <div><span>Input</span><strong>Audio</strong></div>
          <div><span>Representation</span><strong>MFCCs</strong></div>
          <div><span>Model &amp; decoding</span><strong>HMM + Viterbi</strong></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="contribution" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">02 / My contribution</p>
        <h2>Decoding, re-estimation and evaluation.</h2>
        <p>Within the group, I implemented the Viterbi decoder used to evaluate the development set. It operates in the log domain so that long products of small probabilities become stable sums rather than underflowing to zero.</p>
        <p>For an observation sequence <em>O</em> and word model <em>λ</em>, decoding searches for the state sequence with the greatest joint probability:</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="X star equals arg max over X of P of O comma X given lambda">
          X<sup>*</sup> = arg max<sub>X</sub> P(O, X | λ)
          <span class="equation-label">1</span>
        </div>
        <p>The dynamic-programming recurrence stores the best log score reaching state <em>j</em> at frame <em>t</em>. Working with logarithms turns probability products into sums:</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="delta j at t equals max over i of delta i at t minus 1 plus log a i j, plus log b j of o t">
          δ<sub>j</sub>(t) = max<sub>i</sub> [δ<sub>i</sub>(t − 1) + log a<sub>ij</sub>] + log b<sub>j</sub>(o<sub>t</sub>)
          <span class="equation-label">2</span>
        </div>
        <p>Each emission score is the log density of a diagonal multivariate Gaussian. The mean models the expected MFCC vector for a state, while the variance describes the allowed spread around it:</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="log Gaussian emission probability for observation o t in state j">
          log b<sub>j</sub>(o<sub>t</sub>) = −½ [D log(2π) + Σ<sub>d</sub> log σ²<sub>jd</sub> + Σ<sub>d</sub> (o<sub>td</sub> − μ<sub>jd</sub>)² / σ²<sub>jd</sub>]
          <span class="equation-label">3</span>
        </div>
        <p>I also implemented the statistics accumulation and parameter re-estimation stages of Baum–Welch. These functions aggregate state occupancy and transition responsibilities, then update the Gaussian means, variances and transition probabilities while preserving the model’s left-to-right topology.</p>
        <p>The expectation step combines the forward and backward passes to estimate how likely each state was at each frame:</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="gamma t i equals alpha t i beta t i divided by the sum over k of alpha t k beta t k">
          γ<sub>t</sub>(i) = α<sub>t</sub>(i)β<sub>t</sub>(i) / Σ<sub>k</sub> α<sub>t</sub>(k)β<sub>t</sub>(k)
          <span class="equation-label">4</span>
        </div>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="xi t i j proportional to alpha t minus 1 i times a i j times b j of o t times beta t j">
          ξ<sub>t</sub>(i,j) ∝ α<sub>t−1</sub>(i) a<sub>ij</sub> b<sub>j</sub>(o<sub>t</sub>) β<sub>t</sub>(j)
          <span class="equation-label">5</span>
        </div>
        <p>The maximisation step then converts those soft assignments into updated emission parameters:</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="mu j equals the gamma weighted mean of observation vectors">
          μ̂<sub>j</sub> = Σ<sub>t</sub> γ<sub>t</sub>(j)o<sub>t</sub> / Σ<sub>t</sub> γ<sub>t</sub>(j)
          <span class="equation-label">6</span>
        </div>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="sigma squared j equals the gamma weighted variance with a variance floor">
          σ̂²<sub>j</sub> = max(Σ<sub>t</sub> γ<sub>t</sub>(j)(o<sub>t</sub> − μ̂<sub>j</sub>)² / Σ<sub>t</sub> γ<sub>t</sub>(j), σ²<sub>floor</sub>)
          <span class="equation-label">7</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>My work covered:</strong> Viterbi likelihood scoring, EM statistics accumulation, HMM parameter re-estimation, per-epoch development evaluation, and the variance-floor experiments.</p></div>
        <p>Integrating recognition accuracy into the training loop made it possible to watch likelihood and task performance together. Accuracy improved quickly during the early epochs and then levelled off, consistent with stable EM convergence.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/speech-recognition/dev-accuracy.png" alt="Development-set speech recognition accuracy rising and stabilising over training epochs" width="1185" height="773" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 1. Development accuracy monitored during HMM training. Source: group report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="experiment" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">03 / Variance-floor experiment</p>
        <h2>Finding the boundary between overfitting and underfitting.</h2>
        <p>A variance floor prevents the diagonal Gaussian emissions from collapsing around a small collection of MFCC frames. I retrained the models across floor scales from 0.01 to 15 and measured both development and evaluation accuracy.</p>
        <div class="equation" role="math" aria-label="variance floor equals scale s times the global variance">
          σ²<sub>floor</sub> = s · σ²<sub>global</sub>
          <span class="equation-label">8</span>
        </div>
        <p>Very small floors produced development accuracy around 85–87%, but less than 30% on evaluation data: the narrow Gaussians fitted the development speakers closely and generalised poorly. Increasing the floor improved evaluation accuracy, which peaked at 43.80% across several settings. Very large floors reduced both scores as the model became too constrained.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/speech-recognition/variance-floor.png" alt="Line chart showing development and evaluation accuracy across HMM variance-floor scales" width="1166" height="752" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 2. The variance floor exposes a clear generalisation trade-off. Source: group report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Variance scale</th><th>Dev accuracy</th><th>Evaluation accuracy</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0.01</td><td>84.85%</td><td>28.10%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0.20</td><td>86.97%</td><td>32.23%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>1.20</td><td>80.00%</td><td>43.80%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2.80</td><td>80.61%</td><td>43.80%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>15.00</td><td>52.12%</td><td>34.71%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="results" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">04 / Results</p>
        <h2>A working recogniser with visible limits.</h2>
        <div class="stat-grid">
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>43.80%</strong><span>best supplied evaluation-set accuracy</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>11</strong><span>isolated words in the vocabulary</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>8</strong><span>left-to-right states per word model</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>13</strong><span>MFCC coefficients per audio frame</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>The confusion matrix shows why this constrained problem is still difficult. Closely related vowel sounds—such as “hud”, “hod”, “hood” and “hard”—are easily confused when training data and speaker diversity are limited.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/speech-recognition/evaluation-confusion.png" alt="Confusion matrix for the eleven-word supplied speech evaluation set" width="642" height="541" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 3. Supplied evaluation-set confusion matrix. Source: group report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Performance fell to roughly 9.5% on a small collection of custom recordings. Differences in speakers, microphones, background noise and speaking rate exposed the system’s limited robustness. That unfavourable result was useful: correct implementation does not guarantee generalisation when the statistical model and dataset are both small.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="learning" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">05 / What I learned</p>
        <h2>Numerical stability is part of the model.</h2>
        <p class="lead">The project made abstract algorithms concrete: every modelling assumption appeared somewhere in the resulting errors.</p>
        <p>Log-domain decoding prevented underflow. Variance floors controlled whether emission distributions memorised or generalised. The left-to-right topology encoded a useful assumption about speech progression, but diagonal single-Gaussian states could not fully separate similar sounds or adapt to new speakers.</p>
        <p>Natural next steps would include cepstral mean and variance normalisation, more varied training speakers, Gaussian mixtures, or a hybrid neural acoustic model.</p>
      </section>
